OpenChronology server for macOS (Homebrew)
==========================================

Install
-------

    brew install openchronology/tap/openchronology-server
    openchronology-setup

Homebrew installs what the server needs: Node.js 24 (node@24), PostgreSQL 17, GMP and SQLite,
and builds the SQLite converter and the pgmp extension for this Mac (Rust and CMake are needed
only while building). Homebrew's PostgreSQL and the server run as you. openchronology-setup:

  - starts PostgreSQL 17 with `brew services` if it is not running;
  - writes $(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology/openchronology.env (readable only by you), with
    a new AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY and the database at postgresql://<you>@/openchronology;
  - creates the openchronology database, with the bundled pgmp extension;
  - migrates it (openchronology-migrate) and starts the `openchronology-server` service,
    which listens on 127.0.0.1:5173 and starts again when you log in.

openchronology-setup is safe to run again.

Configure
---------

Edit $(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology/openchronology.env, then
`brew services restart openchronology-server`:

  - APP_ORIGIN: the public HTTPS origin users reach, without a trailing slash. The server
    refuses a plain http:// origin other than localhost; on a trusted private network
    without a proxy, set ALLOW_INSECURE_ORIGIN=1 and HOST=0.0.0.0 to serve
    http://<name>:5173 directly. The server logs to $(brew --prefix)/var/log/openchronology.log;
    if the website reports "Server misconfigured", the reason is there.
  - RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM for account email, delivered by `openchronology-mail`
    while it runs (in a terminal, or your own launchd agent).
  - ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD for the first administrator,
    created by `openchronology-migrate`. The password is used once; to sign in with it again
    (or with a new one written there), run `openchronology-reset-admin-password`.
  - Sign-in providers, human verification, quotas: see the comments in the file and
    authentication.md, administration.md in this directory.

Back up AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY apart from the database: without it, enrolled authenticator
secrets cannot be decrypted. Do not change it once accounts exist.

Serve it publicly through an HTTPS reverse proxy (brew install caddy). With Caddy:

    timescale.example {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }

Caddy 2.5 and later replace an incoming X-Forwarded-For with the client address, so set
TRUST_PROXY=1 (with another proxy, only if it does the same; see README.md).

The syslog receiver (syslog.md), `openchronology-syslog`, reads
$(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology/syslog.env. `och-import-logs` imports historic log files
(log-import.md): `och-import-logs --help`.

Upgrade
-------

`brew upgrade openchronology-server` keeps the configuration and the database. Then:

  1. Compare the configuration with this version's template:
         diff -u $(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology/openchronology.env \
                 $(brew --prefix)/share/openchronology/openchronology.env.example
  2. Back up:  $(brew --prefix postgresql@17)/bin/pg_dump -h /tmp openchronology > openchronology.sql
  3. openchronology-migrate, which migrates the database and restarts the server.

Remove
------

Homebrew installed PostgreSQL 17 for the server, so uninstalling the server removes it too,
unless you ask Homebrew to keep it (its databases, in $(brew --prefix)/var/postgresql@17,
remain either way). To keep PostgreSQL and the database running:

    brew install postgresql@17
    brew services stop openchronology-server
    brew uninstall openchronology-server

The configuration (including AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY) and the database remain; delete them with:

    rm -rf $(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology $(brew --prefix)/var/openchronology
    $(brew --prefix postgresql@17)/bin/dropdb -h /tmp openchronology

Layout
------

  $(brew --prefix)/opt/openchronology-server/libexec   the application and och-convert
  $(brew --prefix)/etc/openchronology                  configuration (created by setup)
  $(brew --prefix)/share/openchronology                configuration templates of this version
  $(brew --prefix)/lib/postgresql@17, share/postgresql@17   pgmp
